  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\n- Small rain chances through Tue; Rain Likely Tue night/Wed\n\nNo changes to previous thinking in terms of overall expectations.\nAreas northwest of Grand Rapids, particularly around Ludington, \ncould see precipitation beginning early Tuesday morning shortly\nafter midnight. However this precipitation is expected to remain \nout of most of southwest Lower Michigan.\n\nThe best chances for rain will be late Tuesday night and Wednesday.\nBoth SPC and machine learning guidance suggests that isolated \nthunderstorms are possible, which seems reasonable given that \nextremely strong integrated water vapor transport (based on the \nECMWF ensemble) and isentropic ascent will aid in elevated \nconvective destabilization. Elevated stability-altering processes\nlike these don't tend to be parameterized well by global models, \nincluding the GFS, so this is where machine learning guidance can \nhave an advantage by leveraging analogs associated with the \noverall moisture and kinematic environment. \n\nForecast precipitation totals continue to decrease with successive\nmodel runs. Instantaneous rainfall rates could be impressive; this\nwould be quite concerning if the expected warm cloud depth was \ngreater. Rain totals should be kept in check by this being a \nprogressively moving system.\n\nThe lower expected rainfall appears to be related to the trough\ntransitioning into a cutoff low over the midwest. At the same \ntime, at the southern extent of the deteriorating trough, there \nlooks to be a cyclonic wave break over the Lower Mississippi \nValley associated with a separate upper PV max. Hence, the best \nforcing for ascent and resultant precipitation amounts now look to\noccur in the Gulf Coast states and TN Valley area. This also \nwould reorient the low level moisture transport vectors to point \nmore east of our area via low level PV generation associated with \nlatent heat release in the precipitation area.\n\nThe final thing to note about Tuesday is that it will be extraordinarily\nwarm with highs now expected to be in the mid 70s. Guidance continues\nto increase highs during this time. Wednesday also looks warm \nwith highs approaching 70s.\n\n\n- Cooler with rain chances Thursday and Friday\n\nWe may have a break in rain Wednesday night, as noted previously,\nbut this is neither certain nor likely to be long duration. This \nis because the expected dry slot may miss us to the east. In any \ncase, colder lower tropospheric air pours into the area Thursday \nnight into Friday with 850mb temperatures dropping close to 0C. \nThis will yield delta-T values in the lower/mid teens which is \nsufficient for lake effect rain. Based on wind direction,\nprecipitation will be primarily across far southwest Lower MI.\n\n\n- Limited forecast certainty this weekend\n\nThe upper pattern during this time looks wobbly and complex with\nephemeral cutoff lows rotating through a broader trough over the \nGreat Lakes. With steep lapse rates underneath the colder air \naloft, it would be easy to squeeze out rain showers from even \nmodest upper PV forcing. The latest guidance still does keep \nSaturday dry and Sunday a little wetter, but given the extremely \npoor predictability with this pattern, the weekend forecast will \nbe subject to change a lot during the coming week.",
